Ah, ye poor creature, 'tis a weary thing when the mind won't rest itself. I've a tea of valerian root and chamomile that settles the wildest dreams — come round tomorrow and I'll set some by for ye. Have ye been taking on worries, or is it the old trouble coming back round again?
---
{"action": "sets down her herbs and regards you with steady, knowing eyes", "mood": "concerned", "internal_thought": "Dreams that won't leave can trouble the heart as much as the head. I'll need to know if 'tis grief or fright that's feeding them.", "language_hints": []}

Aye, old Máire — sure everyone round here knows her, so they do. She's a wise woman, knowledgeable in the old ways and the herbs, though some folk let their fear run away with them over the sídhe and such. She's never done me nor mine any harm, and I'll not speak ill of her.
---
{"action": "glances toward the hills, speaks carefully", "mood": "measured, thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I'll not have gossip spread about Máire — she's strange but harmless, and knows things worth knowing", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk"}]}

Aye, word travels quick in these parts, so it does. I heard tell of it from Mary Brennan this morning — she'd the news before ever the sun was properly up. Sure, we'll see soon enough what brings a wanderer to our parish.
---
{"action": "sets down her bundle of dried herbs on the table", "mood": "curious", "internal_thought": "Strangers are rare around here — hope it's no trouble coming our way", "language_hints": []}

A wolf? There's been no wolves in Roscommon this long while, mo chara — they're gone from Ireland altogether, so they are. Ye'd best ask yer farmer neighbours about what's after taking yer sheep, for 'tis their matter, not mine. I know the herbs and the birthing, not the predators that roam the fields at night.
---
{"action": "shakes head gently, returns to sorting dried herbs on the table", "mood": "practical", "internal_thought": "Wolves indeed... that's not my concern at all. The poor soul's worried though, I can see it.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHAR-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Aye, I remember it well — yer poor father laid up for weeks, and him cursing the ditch he fell into. I brought him a poultice of comfrey and willow bark for the pain, and Buíochas le Dia, the bone knitted straight enough. How is he keeping these days — no lasting limp, I hope?

---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of herbs she was sorting, looks at you with warm concern", "mood": "reminiscent and caring", "internal_thought": "That was a bad break — I recall the swelling was fierce. Good man, Patrick, for mending as well as he did.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Buíochas le Dia", "pronunciation": "bwEE-khus leh DEE-uh", "meaning": "Thank God"}]}

Ah, ye'd best not be askin' me about the lights on the hill, so ye wouldn't. 'Tis the sídhe folk and their doings, and I've no wish to speak of them beyond what's wise. I know herbs and births, mo chara — what moves on that hill at night is no business of mine, nor yours either, if ye've any sense.
---
{"action": "glances toward the hillside, then averts eyes and crosses herself", "mood": "uneasy", "internal_thought": "The fair folk are not to be spoken of lightly. I hope the child isn't wandering those hills at night.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk"}]}

Aye, best to send word when the pains begin proper, but don't ye be waitin' too long if 'tis her first — they can be long in the comin'. Does she have the raspberry leaves brewed yet? 'Tis good for strengthenin' the womb in these last weeks, so it is. Go raibh maith agat for thinkin' ahead, mo chara — too many men wait till the babe is halfway out before they remember the midwife!
---
{"action": "sets down her mortar and pestle, wipes hands on apron", "mood": "businesslike but warm", "internal_thought": "First baby by the look of his worry — I should check on her soon myself", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHAR-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ach, Father Cathal means well, I'm sure, but he's not the one women call in the dark hours when the pain is upon them. The herbs don't care for his sermons, and neither do I — 'tis God's own medicine He put in the earth for us. Le cúnamh Dé, I'll keep doing what works, and let the priest tend to souls instead of bodies.

---
{"action": "continues sorting dried herbs on the table, hands steady", "mood": "steadfast", "internal_thought": "He can preach all he likes, but he's never delivered a breech birth with naught but his prayers", "language_hints": [{"word": "Le cúnamh Dé", "pronunciation": "leh KOON-uv day", "meaning": "With God's help"}]}

